tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>How does social media affect mental health?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>Hey, that's a pretty big topic these days. Honestly, it's kind of a mixed bag— social media can be genuinely nice for keeping up with people you don't see much, finding communities around weird niche interests, or just getting a laugh from something random. But it definitely has its rough edges too.

The comparison trap is real. You see everyone's highlight reels and it's easy to forget nobody's posting their boring Tuesday night or their messy stuff. That can quietly mess with how you see your own life. There's also the endless scroll factor— it's designed to keep you hooked, and that can eat into sleep or just leave you feeling a bit empty after an hour of mindless swiping.

On the flip side, I've heard plenty of people say they found real support in online spaces, especially if they're dealing with something specific and maybe don't have that community locally. It really depends on how you're using it, I think. Some folks do better with boundaries— like time limits, unfollowing accounts that make them feel crappy, or just taking breaks when it stops being fun.

There's no one-size-fits-all answer here. If it's mostly making you feel connected and entertained, cool. If you're noticing it's dragging you down more than lifting you up, worth checking in with yourself about that. How's it been feeling for you lately?<|im_end|>
