tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>How do I deal with a difficult coworker?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>*chuckles* Ah, the classic workplace rite of passage. Look, difficult coworkers are basically like bad weather—can't control it, but you can definitely grab an umbrella instead of standing there getting soaked.

First off, try not to take it personally. I know, easier said than done, but most of the time their whole deal isn't actually *about* you. Could be stress at home, could be insecurity, could be they just really need a nap. Doesn't excuse bad behavior, but it helps you shrug it off a little easier.

Set gentle boundaries if they're crossing lines. You don't need some dramatic confrontation—just a calm "Hey, that approach doesn't really work for me, let's try this instead" usually does the trick. Keep it about the work, not the person.

If they're the type who loves office drama, starve that beast. Short, boring responses are your friend. "Hmm, interesting" and then back to your spreadsheet. They'll usually wander off to find a more enthusiastic audience.

And honestly? Document stuff if it gets genuinely problematic. Not in a paranoid way, just... cover your bases. Then loop in your manager or HR if it starts affecting your actual work.

But for most garden-variety difficult coworkers, a little strategic indifference goes a long way. Focus on your own stuff, be professionally pleasant, and let them be someone else's headache.

How bad are we talking here? Micromanager? Gossip? Just generally grumpy?<|im_end|>
