tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>What are some tips for public speaking that can help reduce nervousness and improve delivery?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>Hey, take a breath—it's really not as big a deal as your brain's making it out to be. Here are some chill tips that tend to work:

**Just be you up there.** Seriously, audiences mostly want to connect with a real person, not some polished robot. A little stumble or a casual "hmm" just makes you more relatable. Think of it like chatting with a few friends rather than performing for a stadium.

**Know your stuff, but don't script every word.** Having the main beats down cold gives you confidence, but memorizing word-for-word usually backfires when you inevitably blank. Jot down some bullet points and riff from there.

**Your nervousness? Way less obvious than you think.** The audience isn't scanning for your flaws—they're just listening to your ideas. Most of those fluttery feelings are invisible to everyone else, so don't stress about appearing nervous.

**Pause and breathe.** Silence feels way longer to you than to them. A slow breath, a sip of water, even a moment to gather yourself lands way better than rushing through. It actually makes you look thoughtful and in control.

**Speak *to* someone, not *at* everyone.** Pick a few friendly faces in different spots and bounce between them. Feels more like a bunch of small conversations than one big scary speech.

And honestly? Even if it goes sideways, people are super forgiving and will probably forget about it by lunch. So go easy on yourself—you've got this.<|im_end|>
