tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>Imagine a world where the concept of time is viewed differently, allowing for a more relaxed pace of life; describe the societal changes that would need to occur to achieve this.<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>In a world like that, I think the biggest shift would be simply letting go of the idea that faster always means better. You'd see workplaces redesigning themselves around outcomes rather than hours clocked—maybe people work intensely for a few hours, then take long midday breaks without anyone side-eyeing them. Deadlines would still exist, but they'd be more like gentle agreements than threats hanging over your head.

Education would probably stretch out way more. Instead of cramming everything into four rushed years, you might study a subject for a decade, dipping in and out as life happens. And retirement? The whole concept might blur into just... living, with work and rest mixed together throughout your whole life rather than front-loading everything.

Public spaces would change too. Cities would slow down—more pedestrian streets, fewer cars rushing through, benches and parks designed for lingering rather than passing through. Even technology would adapt; maybe notifications come in batches a few times a day instead of constantly poking at you.

The tricky part is that this requires a massive mindset shift about scarcity. We'd have to genuinely believe there's enough time for what actually matters, which is surprisingly hard when you're used to hustling. But honestly? Once people got used to it, I bet most would wonder how they ever lived the old way.<|im_end|>
